## Solution

For this challenge, I followed the proposed
optimization of storing the instruction pointer
in a local variable inside the VM's run() function.

In the original implementation, each CallFrame
contains an instruction pointer, which tracks
the next bytecode instruction to execute.

The VM accesses this pointer using:

```c
frame->ip
```

Since the VM reads instructions constantly, this
access happens repeatedly during execution.

To reduce this overhead, I would cache the
instruction pointer in a local variable and
use it directly when reading bytecode.

The compiler may be able to keep this local
variable in a CPU register, reducing repeated
memory accesses.

## 1. Store ip in a Local Variable

In vm.c, modify the beginning of run():

```c
static InterpretResult run() {
  CallFrame* frame = &vm.frames[vm.frameCount - 1];

  register uint8_t* ip = frame->ip;
```

The new ip variable stores the current
instruction pointer.

The register keyword suggests that the C
compiler should keep the variable in a
CPU register when possible.

Modern compilers are free to make their own
register allocation decisions, so the
keyword does not guarantee this optimization.

## 2. Update the Bytecode Reading Macros

Originally, the VM reads bytecode through
the CallFrame:

```c
#define READ_BYTE() (*frame->ip++)
```

I would change the macros to use the local
instruction pointer:

```c
#define READ_BYTE() (*ip++)

#define READ_SHORT() \
    (ip += 2, \
    (uint16_t)((ip[-2] << 8) | ip[-1]))
```

READ_BYTE() now retrieves an instruction
and advances the local ip variable.

READ_SHORT() reads a two-byte operand
and advances ip by two bytes.

READ_CONSTANT() can continue using
READ_BYTE() to retrieve its operand.

This reduces the need to repeatedly access
the instruction pointer through frame->ip.

## 3. Update Jump Instructions

The VM uses jump instructions to change
the flow of execution.

These instructions must now update the
local ip variable instead of frame->ip.

In vm.c:

```c
case OP_JUMP: {
  uint16_t offset = READ_SHORT();
  ip += offset;
  break;
}

case OP_JUMP_IF_FALSE: {
  uint16_t offset = READ_SHORT();

  if (isFalsey(peek(0))) {
    ip += offset;
  }

  break;
}

case OP_LOOP: {
  uint16_t offset = READ_SHORT();
  ip -= offset;
  break;
}
```

OP_JUMP moves forward unconditionally.

OP_JUMP_IF_FALSE moves forward only
when the condition is falsey.

OP_LOOP moves backward to repeat
a section of bytecode.

These instructions continue working
the same way, but they now modify
the cached instruction pointer.

## 4. Synchronize ip During Function Calls

One important issue with this optimization
is keeping the cached instruction pointer
synchronized with the current CallFrame.

When the VM calls a function, it switches
to a different CallFrame.

Before making that switch, the current ip
must be saved so execution can resume
at the correct location when the
called function returns.

Update OP_CALL:

```c
case OP_CALL: {
  int argCount = READ_BYTE();

  // Save the caller's instruction pointer.
  frame->ip = ip;

  if (!callValue(peek(argCount), argCount)) {
    return INTERPRET_RUNTIME_ERROR;
  }

  // Load the current frame and its ip.
  frame = &vm.frames[vm.frameCount - 1];
  ip = frame->ip;

  break;
}
```

Saving frame->ip before the call is
important because the VM needs to
remember where execution should
continue afterward.

If callValue() creates a new CallFrame,
the VM loads that frame's instruction
pointer into the local variable.

If a native function executes without
creating a new CallFrame, reloading
the existing frame is still valid.

## 5. Synchronize ip When Returning

When a function returns, the VM
removes the current CallFrame and
resumes execution in its caller.

The instruction pointer must therefore
be loaded from the caller's CallFrame.

The relevant OP_RETURN implementation
becomes:

```c
case OP_RETURN: {
  Value result = pop();
  vm.frameCount--;

  if (vm.frameCount == 0) {
    pop();
    return INTERPRET_OK;
  }

  vm.stackTop = frame->slots;
  push(result);

  // Restore the caller's CallFrame.
  frame = &vm.frames[vm.frameCount - 1];

  // Restore the caller's instruction pointer.
  ip = frame->ip;

  break;
}
```

This allows execution to continue
immediately after the original
function call.

Without this step, the VM might
continue reading bytecode from the
wrong function.

## 6. Synchronize ip Before Runtime Errors

The instruction pointer is also
important when reporting errors.

Chapter 24 explains that the VM
uses each CallFrame's instruction
pointer to identify the source
line where an error occurred.

Since the local ip variable may
have advanced beyond frame->ip,
the current value must be stored
before reporting a runtime error.

For example, in OP_GET_GLOBAL:

```c
case OP_GET_GLOBAL: {
  ObjString* name = READ_STRING();
  Value value;

  if (!tableGet(&vm.globals, name, &value)) {
    frame->ip = ip;

    runtimeError("Undefined variable '%s'.",
                 name->chars);

    return INTERPRET_RUNTIME_ERROR;
  }

  push(value);
  break;
}
```

The same synchronization is required
before other runtime errors.

Examples include:

- Accessing an undefined global.
- Assigning to an undefined global.
- Performing arithmetic on invalid types.
- Adding incompatible values.
- Negating a non-number.

The BINARY_OP macro must also save
the current ip before reporting a
numeric operand error.

For example:

```c
#define BINARY_OP(valueType, op) \
  do { \
    if (!IS_NUMBER(peek(0)) || \
        !IS_NUMBER(peek(1))) { \
      frame->ip = ip; \
      runtimeError("Operands must be numbers."); \
      return INTERPRET_RUNTIME_ERROR; \
    } \
    double b = AS_NUMBER(pop()); \
    double a = AS_NUMBER(pop()); \
    push(valueType(a op b)); \
  } while (false)
```

Other runtimeError() call sites inside
run() should follow the same rule.

These extra assignments happen only
on error paths, so they do not add
unnecessary work during successful
arithmetic and variable access.

## 7. Update Debug Instruction Tracing

If DEBUG_TRACE_EXECUTION is enabled,
the disassembler should use the
current local instruction pointer.

The relevant call becomes:

```c
disassembleInstruction(
    &frame->function->chunk,
    (int)(ip - frame->function->chunk.code));
```

This allows the debug output to show
the instruction currently being executed
rather than using a possibly outdated
instruction pointer stored in the frame.

## 8. Benchmark 1: Recursive Fibonacci

The first benchmark focuses on
function calls and returns.

```lox
fun fib(n) {
  if (n < 2) return n;

  return fib(n - 2) + fib(n - 1);
}

var start = clock();

print fib(30);

print clock() - start;
```

Expected Fibonacci result:

```text
832040
```

The final printed number is the
elapsed CPU time in seconds.

That timing varies depending on the
machine and compiler settings.

I chose this benchmark because recursive
Fibonacci generates many function calls
and returns.

This makes it useful for testing both
instruction pointer access and the
synchronization required when switching
CallFrames.

## 9. Benchmark 2: Loop Execution

The second benchmark tests repeated
bytecode execution with fewer function
calls.

```lox
var start = clock();

var total = 0;
var i = 0;

while (i < 1000000) {
  total = total + i;
  i = i + 1;
}

print total;
print clock() - start;
```

Expected sum:

```text
499999500000
```

The final printed value is again the
measured CPU time.

I chose this benchmark because loops
repeatedly execute instructions such
as OP_GET_GLOBAL, OP_SET_GLOBAL,
OP_ADD, and OP_LOOP.

It helps evaluate whether caching ip
improves ordinary bytecode execution
in addition to function-heavy programs.

## 10. Benchmarking Method

To evaluate the optimization fairly,
I would compare two versions of clox:

1. The original Chapter 24 VM using
   frame->ip directly.

2. The optimized VM using a local
   register ip variable.

Both versions should be compiled
with the same compiler and
optimization settings.

Debug instruction tracing should
be disabled for the timing tests.

I would run each benchmark several
times and compare the median
execution times to reduce noise.

The percentage improvement can
be calculated as:

```text
Improvement (%) =
    ((original time - optimized time)
     / original time) * 100
```

## 11. Performance Results and Expectations

Caching the instruction pointer in a local C variable
could improve performance by reducing repeated
accesses through the CallFrame pointer.

Since the VM reads the instruction pointer frequently
during bytecode execution, reducing this overhead
may help the interpreter execute instructions more
efficiently.

However, the actual performance improvement would
depend on several factors, including the compiler,
hardware, optimization settings, and the type of
program being executed.

The Fibonacci benchmark would help evaluate the
optimization during frequent function calls and
returns, while the loop benchmark would help
measure its effect during repeated bytecode execution.

I have not run these benchmarks or collected
actual timing measurements, so I cannot determine
how much faster the optimized implementation
would be.

## 12. Is the Added Complexity Worth It?

I think this optimization could be worthwhile
because the instruction pointer is accessed
so frequently during execution.

However, the optimization also makes the VM
more complicated because the cached instruction
pointer must be synchronized during function
calls, returns, and runtime errors.

If that synchronization is handled incorrectly,
the VM could execute the wrong instructions
or produce inaccurate stack traces.

Overall, I think the optimization is worth
exploring, but I would want to run benchmarks
before deciding whether the performance benefit
justifies the additional complexity.

## 12. Is the Added Complexity Worth It?

I think this optimization is worthwhile
because the instruction pointer is
accessed during almost every
bytecode instruction.

Even a relatively small improvement
to such a frequently used operation
can have a noticeable effect on
overall performance.

The main disadvantage is that the
compiler and VM code become slightly
more complicated.

We must remember to synchronize the
cached instruction pointer whenever
the active CallFrame changes and before
runtime errors are reported.

If this synchronization is handled
incorrectly, the VM could resume
execution at the wrong instruction
or produce inaccurate stack traces.

However, the changes are still
reasonably localized to run().

Based on the proposed solution's
reported performance improvement,
I think the optimization offers
a reasonable trade-off.

## Conclusion

For this challenge, I optimized the
proposed clox VM by caching the
instruction pointer in a local
register variable.

I updated the instruction-reading
macros, jump instructions, function
calls, returns, runtime error handling,
and debug tracing to use the
cached instruction pointer.

I also designed two benchmarks:
recursive Fibonacci and repeated
loop execution.

The provided solution reports an
approximately 8.5% speedup for
Fibonacci on its author's machine,
but I have not independently
verified that result.

Overall, I think this optimization
is worth considering because it
targets an operation performed
throughout bytecode execution.

